    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "d3920c47e3121e0c5148c058f65c7766",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1730,
    "chunk_text": "The operator has further said that fixed connections are increasingly used for fibre-based Internet access, where changing broadband providers is generally not dependent on retaining the associated telephone number in the same manner as in mobile services.\n\nAccordingly, the fixed operator has proposed implementing MNP first and considering FNP subsequently, subject to demonstrated consumer demand, a detailed cost-benefit assessment and agreement among relevant parties to amend the original simultaneous implementation scope.\n\nTherefore, a discussion between the Ministry of Digital Economy, the TRCSL and the operators are currently ongoing to identify a suitable way forward for the implementation of Mobile Number Portability in Sri Lanka. If the telecom industry does not come to an agreement by the end of September, the Telecommunication Regulatory Commission will proceed by way of direction under the Sri Lanka Telecommunications Act and the conditions of the operators\u2019 licences. The Government will, if necessary, commission Mobile Number Portability first, ahead of fixed connections, so that the greater majority of subscribers are not made to wait on a dispute that does not concern them.\n\n1933 1934\n\n\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0db8 \u0dc3\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\n2. The TRCSL has taken steps to finalize the drafting of rules and guidelines in coordination with the operators, which could be published upon the finalization of implementation date.\n\nLanka Number Portability Services (Guarantee) Limited - LNPS - has taken steps to procure the technical solution and the Letter of Intent is already being issued, but the agreement and the PO are on hold due to the disagreement of one fixed operator in original implementation plan."
