    "chunk_size_bytes": 1558,
    "chunk_text": "3. The annual cigarette consumption declined significantly by 53.1 per cent from approximately 4.1 billion sticks in 2015 to 1.923 billion sticks in 2025. Over the same period, the Government revenue from Excise Duty on cigarettes and Tobacco Tax increased from Rs. 81 billion in 2015 to Rs. 102.7 billion in 2025.\n\nIn addition to the Excise Duty and the Tobacco Tax, cigarettes are subject to the Value Added Tax and a Social Security Contribution Levy of 2.5 per\n\nTo preserve the real value of the Excise Duty over time and prevent erosion of the tax burden due to inflation, an inflation indexation mechanism for cigarette Excise Duty was introduced with effect from 01st January, 2024. Accordingly, the Excise Duty on cigarettes increased by 14 per cent in 2024 and by 5.9 per cent in 2025.\n\nThe illegal importation of cigarettes constitutes an offence under the Customs Ordinance and the Tobacco Tax Act. In Sri Lanka, the Customs take necessary action to detect and seize such illicit imports at the country\u2019s airports and seaports and to initiate legal proceedings against the persons involved in such offences. In addition, the Department of Excise, the Sri Lanka Navy and the Sri Lanka Police undertake enforcement and surveillance activities, including inspections, investigations, raids and intelligence-sharing, to prevent illegal importation, distribution, and sale of illicit cigarettes. Those agencies work in coordination with each other to combat the illicit tobacco trade and to ensure compliance with the applicable laws and regulations."
